MongoDB queries practice questions with solutions:

Use the below website for querying pratice and navigate to the template "Multiple Collections": https://mongoplayground.net/
Note that this playground supports querying only the following commands: Update, Find, Aggregate

Query pratice questions-Basic:

1. Find all the almonds orders from the orders collection:

db.orders.find({
  "item": "almonds"
})

2. Update the instock quantity of cashews in inventory collection to 80:

db.inventory.update({
  "sku": "cashews"
},
{
  $set: {
    "instock": 80
  }
})

3. Find the item almonds in inventory and display only it's name and instock quantity:

db.inventory.find({
  "sku": "almonds"
},
{
  "instock": 1,
  "sku": 1,
  "_id": 0
})

4. Find the items which have more than 100 instock quantity:

db.inventory.find({
  "instock": {
    $gt: 100
  }
})

5.Querying below 10 items orders: 
Find the orders whose quantity greater than or equal to 1 and lesser than or equal to 10:
(here you can manually update the document with _id=3 in orders collection with the below details in the left hand side pane) and then do the querying:

{
      "_id": 3,
      "item":"beans",
      "price":50,
      "quantity":15
    }

  Solution:

  db.orders.find({
  "quantity": {
    $gte: 1,
    $lt: 10
  }
})

6. Making use of the below sample data, manually update the warehouse locations in all the documents in Inventory collection:

 "wh_location": {
    "city": "bangalore",
    "state": "karnataka",
    "country": "india"
  }

  and then query the products instock for the city "Hubli":

  db.inventory.find({
  "wh_location.city": "hubli"
})


Query pratice questions-Intermediate:

7. Find the inventory documents where one of the suppliers is 1001 after updating the documents using the below sample data:

"supplier IDs": [
        1001,
        1030
      ]

  Solution:

db.inventory.find({
  "supplier IDs": 1001
})

8. Retrieve orders made in the month of January 2026 by updating the documents using the below sample data in orders collection:

  {
      "_id": 1,
      "item": "almonds",
      "price": 12,
      "quantity": 2,
      "date_ordered": ISODate("2026-01-23"),
    }

Solution:

    db.orders.find({
  "date_ordered": {
    $gte: ISODate("2026-01-01"),
    $lte: ISODate("2026-01-31")
  }
})

9. Calculate the total sales made by aggregating the price*quantity(sales amount) from the orders collection:

db.orders.aggregate({
  $group: {
    _id: null,
    total: {
      $sum: {
        $multiply: [
          "$price",
          "$quantity"
        ]
      }
    }
  }
})

10. Making use of the below sample data, manually update the warehouse locations in all the documents in Inventory collection:

 "wh_location": {
    "city": "bangalore",
    "state": "karnataka",
    "country": "india"
  }

  and then query the products instock for the city "Hubli" and whose instock quantity is greater than 30:

  db.inventory.find({
  "wh_location.city": "hubli",
  "instock": {
    $gte: 30
  }
})











